<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Academic Achievement and Skill Development – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development: How?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present a summary of information depicted in a book or article. Interactive: Participate in group discussions on current events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present a summary of academic advice from an article, highlighting key points and examples (e.g., "The article explains that if you organize your notes, you will study more effectively. It also suggests taking regular breaks.").
Can participate in group discussions about study habits, sharing personal tips and advice (e.g., "I think using flashcards really helps. Rubiela recommended that I should try them for vocabulary practice.").
Can express agreement or disagreement during discussions on academic success strategies, providing reasons for their opinions (e.g., "I agree that setting goals is important because it helps me stay focused." "I don't think studying late at night is effective, since I get too tired.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 45%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images related to academic success (e.g., a student studying, receiving an award, a well-organized desk) and asks students: "What do you see in these pictures?" and "What do these images suggest about academic achievement?" Students briefly brainstorm words or phrases related to 'success,' 'study habits,' or 'goals' from Lesson 1 and 2, sharing orally in pairs for 2 minutes before a whole-class check. For example: "I see a student with good time management."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b> The teacher models key structures and vocabulary for giving and asking for advice, focusing on clear pronunciation. Teacher states: "If I wanted to improve my grades, I would organize my study schedule." "You should try using flashcards for vocabulary development." "What study strategies do you recommend to achieve academic goals?" The teacher also models the pronunciation of 'progress' (noun /prəʊɡrɛs/ vs. verb /prəˈɡrɛs/) and contrastive sounds like /gr/ in 'growth' and /kr/ in 'create,' emphasizing clarity and stress patterns. For example, "Listen: <ins>PRO</ins>gress (noun) vs. pro<ins>GRESS</ins> (verb)."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the target language and concepts.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'You should study,' am I giving an option or a strong command?" (Expected answer: An option/suggestion.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the difference in meaning when I say 'I have good progress' (noun) versus 'I progress well' (verb)?" (Expected answer: Noun means improvement/development, verb means to move forward/advance.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b> Students are provided with a short transcript (approx. 200 words) of an interview titled "Secrets to Success: A University Student's Perspective" which summarizes advice on effective study habits and academic skill development. This text integrates vocabulary like 'achievement,' 'discipline,' 'time management,' 'strategy,' 'feedback,' and uses modals for advice and conditional sentences. This builds upon the vocabulary and context established in Lesson 2.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing and interpreting information, thoughts, feelings, opinions, and experiences through oral interaction in various communicative situations related to academic achievement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the transcript individually.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main purpose of this interview?" (Expected answer: To provide advice on how to be successful academically.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key pieces of advice from the interview (e.g., "organize notes," "seek feedback") with their corresponding benefits (e.g., "better understanding," "continuous improvement").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identify key vocabulary:</b> Students underline all nouns related to academic achievement (e.g., 'achievement', 'skill', 'development', 'goals') and verbs for study actions (e.g., 'improve', 'practice', 'organize') in the text.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice giving and asking for academic advice using the second conditional ("If I were more organized, I would achieve my academic goals."), modals for advice ("You should complete your assignments on time to succeed academically."), and the future simple for first conditional ("If you focus on your studies, you will achieve your academic goals."). The practice integrates target vocabulary (e.g., 'time management,' 'focus,' 'strategy,' 'improvement,' 'discipline').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: Advice Gap-Fill & Speak (5 min):</b> Students work individually to complete sentences with appropriate modals (should, must, can) or second conditional forms. Example: "If I (want) to improve my grades, I (study) harder." (Answer: 'wanted', 'would study'). After filling in, students read their completed sentences aloud to a partner, focusing on the correct pronunciation of stressed words like 'study,' 'grades,' 'improve.'</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Practice: Academic Challenge & Advice Chain (7 min):</b> In groups of three, students role-play. Student A states an academic challenge (e.g., "I always procrastinate on my essays."). Student B gives advice using a modal verb ("You should create a strict plan to avoid procrastination."). Student C gives alternative advice using the second conditional ("If I were you, I would break down my tasks into smaller steps."). They rotate roles after each turn, ensuring everyone practices giving and receiving advice.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: Ask for and Give Specific Advice (8 min):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes down two personal academic challenges they face (e.g., "I struggle with time management," "I find it hard to focus during online classes"). They then take turns asking their partner for advice using questions like, "What study strategies do you recommend to improve my grades?" or "If I were more disciplined, what could I achieve?" The partner responds with comprehensive advice, incorporating vocabulary like 'strategy,' 'organization,' 'motivation,' 'feedback.'</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Task: Group Discussion: "Designing Our Academic Success Roadmap"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students form small groups (4-5 students). Their task is to:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Summarize Article Advice (5 min):</b> Each group collaboratively summarizes the key advice points from the "Secrets to Success" interview transcript (from Stage 2), highlighting 3-4 main strategies. One student from each group will be designated to present this summary. Example: "The article explains that if you organize your notes, you will study more effectively. It also suggests taking regular breaks."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Discuss and Share Personal Tips (8 min):</b> Within their groups, students discuss study habits that have worked for them and share personal tips and advice. They are encouraged to use inclusive language ("Let's share study techniques that have worked for us.") and express agreement or disagreement, providing reasons. Example: "I agree that setting goals is important because it helps me stay focused." "I don't think studying late at night is effective, since I get too tired."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Develop Group Recommendations (5 min):</b> Each group then develops 2-3 new, actionable pieces of advice for academic skill development, combining insights from the article summary and their personal experiences. They must use second conditionals and modals for advice. Example: "If we were more consistent, we would see greater progress." "You must track your performance regularly for effective feedback."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Role:</b> Facilitate discussions, monitor language use (grammar, vocabulary, pronunciation), and offer prompts as needed to encourage deeper interaction and use of target structures. Ensure students are articulating clear, actionable advice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their summarized article advice and their 2-3 group recommendations to the class (1-2 minutes per group). Other groups provide constructive feedback based on clarity and usefulness of the advice. Students are assessed on their individual participation within the group discussion and their ability to present and discuss advice during the brief presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, particularly contrastive sounds (/gr/, /kr/, /g/, /k/) and correct stress patterns for two-syllable words like 'progress' (noun/verb).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and varied use of academic achievement and skill development vocabulary (e.g., 'achievement,' 'strategy,' 'motivation,' 'feedback').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Advice and summaries are clearly articulated and logically presented.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammatical accuracy:</b> Correct use of second conditional for hypothetical advice, modals for advice (should, must, can), and first conditional for cause-effect relationships.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Active participation in group discussions, using inclusive language, and effectively expressing agreement/disagreement.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I use modals and conditionals to give advice in today's discussion? (e.g., 'I used 'should' frequently but need more practice with the second conditional.')"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new study strategy or piece of academic advice I learned from my peers today that I plan to implement?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about summarizing the article's advice or giving clear advice in English during the group discussions?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I continue to improve my speaking skills for academic discussions and presentations?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., student engagement in group discussions, active use of target grammar/vocabulary).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with forming second conditionals or pronouncing specific vocabulary? (e.g., more targeted drills, sentence frames for complex structures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which aspects of the target pronunciation (contrastive sounds, stress patterns) require further attention?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson:</b> The academic advice and strategies discussed and summarized today will serve as the foundation for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where students will write a short advice column or a paragraph detailing their personal academic success plan based on the insights gained from today's speaking activities. They will also practice writing sentences using the target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>